Here's a basic **HTML code snippet for a login page** that includes placeholder features (e.g., username and password fields), and a **simple form submission handler**. You can extend this with backend logic (e.g., fetching user credentials from a database or API) to perform authentication.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Login Page</title>
  <style>
    body {
      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
      background: #f4f4f4;
      text-align: center;
    }

    form {
      max-width: 400px;
      margin: 40px auto;
      background: #fff;
      padding: 20px;
      border-radius: 8px;
      box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
    }

    input[type="text"], input[type="password"] {
      width: 100%;
      padding: 10px;
      margin-bottom: 15px;
      border: 1px solid #ccc;
      border-radius: 4px;
    }

    button {
      padding: 10px 15px;
      font-size: 16px;
      background-color: #007BFF;
      color: white;
      border: none;
      border-radius: 4px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }

    button:hover {
      background-color: #0056b3;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

  <h2>Login Page</h2>

  <form onsubmit="handleLogin(event)">
    <label for="username">Username:</label>
    <input type="text" id="username" name="username" required><br>

    <label for="password">Password:</label>
    <input type="password" id="password" name="password" required><br>

    <button type="submit">Login</button>
  </form>

  <script>
    function handleLogin(event) {
      event.preventDefault();

      // Simulate login logic (you can replace this with actual backend call here)
      alert("Login successful! Credentials: " + document.getElementById('username').value + " - " + document.getElementById('password').value);

      // If you want to fetch credentials from backend, use fetch API:
      // fetch('/login', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({ username: document.getElementById('username').value, password: document.getElementById('password').value }) })
      //   .then(response => response.json())
      //   .then(data => alert("User credentials retrieved: " + data.username))
      //   .catch(error => console.error("Error:", error));
    }
  </script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Notes:
- This is a **basic example**. You can replace the `handleLogin` function with actual logic to fetch user credentials from a backend.
- You can add more features like password validation
